68
00:03:09,090 --> 00:03:09,910
So what's the difference here?

69
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:13,250
So paramedics are something like nurses, right?

70
00:03:13,510 --> 00:03:20,110
That travel in an ambulance and get to an emergency or get to the scene of an accident and help out a person.

71
00:03:20,110 --> 00:03:23,050
So they immobilize the person, they give them oxygen.

72
00:03:23,050 --> 00:03:26,610
They make sure that they stay alive until they reach the hospital.

73
00:03:26,890 --> 00:03:30,690
Okay, so a paramedic, you might hear the word medic, it's the same thing.

74
00:03:31,210 --> 00:03:32,790
A paramedic is like a nurse.

75
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:40,720
They're not a doctor, but they're usually the first person to help when there's been an accident or a disaster.

76
00:03:41,060 --> 00:03:41,960
Right, exactly.

77
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:52,640
So if there is an earthquake and many people are stuck in the buildings, usually paramedics will be the ones on the scene trying to get them out and helping them by applying for first aid.

78
00:03:52,700 --> 00:03:57,120
Well, after that, we have finally some of the injuries that a paramedic might see.

79
00:03:57,540 --> 00:04:01,460
The first injury is usually associated with the head.

80
00:04:01,870 --> 00:04:02,850
This is called a concussion.

81
00:04:03,410 --> 00:04:04,410
You suffered a concussion.

82
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:06,120
It sounds painful.

83
00:04:06,310 --> 00:04:06,990
Yeah, it does.

84
00:04:07,210 --> 00:04:08,890
And actually, it's very common, I think, right?

85
00:04:09,030 --> 00:04:10,270
If you fall and you hit your head.

86
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:11,240
That's right.

87
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:14,500
So a concussion is really when your brain hits your skull.

88
00:04:14,840 --> 00:04:16,760
That's the bone that protects your brain.

89
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:20,180
So really, you're hitting your head really, really hard.

90
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:27,420
And usually, this can have some bad side effects like you lose your memory or you're a little bit confused.

91
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:29,880
It's very common with football players.

92
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:30,740
Yeah, yeah.

93
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:40,200
With any contact sport, if people suffer concussions, and it's just like, imagine when you have a DVD player and you hit it and the disc skips or it goes back.

94
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:41,800
That's kind of what happens to your brain.

95
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:43,000
So you can suffer memory loss.

96
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:44,480
You can even lose your sight temporarily.

97
00:04:44,940 --> 00:04:45,240
That's right.

98
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:50,520
So this can be very serious and obviously should be looked at by a paramedic.

99
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:53,440
We also have another situation that is similarly serious.

100
00:04:54,080 --> 00:04:56,300
It's called a compound fracture.

101
00:04:56,880 --> 00:04:57,160
Okay.

102
00:04:57,660 --> 00:04:59,720
So what is a fracture or two fracture?

103
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:01,640
A fracture is a thing.

104
00:05:01,640 --> 00:05:02,120
It's a break.

105
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:03,020
Okay.

106
00:05:03,440 --> 00:05:05,320
So you could say, I broke my arm.

107
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:07,890
But you could also say, I fractured my arm.

108
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:09,850
This is the more medical term.

109
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:13,240
But of course, there are different ways to break something.

110
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:17,040
And so in medicine, we have a compound fracture.

111
00:05:17,040 --> 00:05:20,990
So a compound fracture is pretty painful and it's a little bit more serious.

112
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:24,560
You have a simple fracture, which is when your bone just breaks.

113
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:29,890
Or a hairline, which means that there's a little thin part where it's broken, but it's not really serious.

114
00:05:30,210 --> 00:05:33,470
In this case, it's your bones actually push towards each other.

115
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:37,060
They kind of form like a little mountain, right?

116
00:05:37,060 --> 00:05:38,490
Or they're on top of each other.

117
00:05:38,790 --> 00:05:42,550
Think about when two cars hit and they kind of crash up into each other.

118
00:05:42,630 --> 00:05:42,770
Exactly.

119
00:05:43,130 --> 00:05:44,230
It's like that, but it's your bone.

120
00:05:44,730 --> 00:05:45,970
So it's extremely painful.

121
00:05:46,210 --> 00:05:46,690
Yeah, yeah.

122
00:05:46,850 --> 00:05:47,630
It's very, very painful.

123
00:05:48,230 --> 00:05:50,950
So the person suffered a concussion and a compound fracture.

124
00:05:51,610 --> 00:05:54,750
Now the friend that he was talking to said, wow, it sounds amazing.

125
00:05:55,170 --> 00:05:56,370
It's really cool that you're doing that.

126
00:05:56,550 --> 00:05:57,490
But you know what?

127
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:00,440
I faint when I see blood.

128
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:00,880
Do you do this?

129
00:06:01,480 --> 00:06:01,640
No.

130
00:06:02,440 --> 00:06:03,440
No, me neither.

131
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:06,800
Well, some people when they see blood, they feel very, very uncomfortable or sick.

132
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:10,980
And so to faint is to fall down on the ground.

133
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:13,500
It's almost like you're falling asleep, but it's not sleep.

134
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:15,240
Have you ever fainted?

135
00:06:15,960 --> 00:06:18,950
I fainted twice in my life.

136
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:21,270
One time I fainted because of heat.

137
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:23,250
It was very, very, very hot.

138
00:06:24,380 --> 00:06:26,240
And I couldn't breathe for a moment.

139
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:27,470
And then I woke up on the ground.

140
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:28,920
Wow.

141
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:29,920
Yeah.

142
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:30,920
Yeah.

143
00:06:30,920 --> 00:06:31,920
That's what happens.

144
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:32,920
Exactly.

145
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:38,640
So yeah, if you ever faint, it's basically that you're just standing there, you're sitting somewhere and all of a sudden everything goes black and you wake up and you're on the ground.

146
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:40,690
And you feel very, very weak before that.

147
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:42,490
And very pale, I think as well.

148
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:46,350
Yeah, your blood goes away from your brain and you pass out.

149
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:48,170
It's a very strange sensation.

150
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:52,090
Because then everyone else around you, they know what happened.

151
00:06:52,680 --> 00:06:56,130
They saw you and so they're looking at you like you're crazy or maybe sick.

152
00:06:57,480 --> 00:06:57,750
To faint.

153
00:06:58,480 --> 00:06:59,480
All right.

154
00:06:59,480 --> 00:07:00,640
So that's to faint.

